on/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0dca618-d80d-52a6-906f-74aa4b9fbda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{6}r+\\frac{1}{6}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359319} {:answer/id #uuid "f32865da-d1db-55cb-b5a1-224864b96bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{6}{11}r+\\frac{5}{11}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359320} {:answer/id #uuid "b84a4cfa-d29c-56c0-82d6-46f04fcabce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=r+\\frac{5}{6}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359321} {:answer/id #uuid "0445ced9-725c-53d8-8045-c4170dad542a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{11}r+\\frac{6}{11}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359322} {:answer/id #uuid "581474e8-b023-5ee4-9dd4-6a1b95149d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{1}{11}r+s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0445ced9-725c-53d8-8045-c4170dad542a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{11}r+\\frac{6}{11}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359322} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac26a9a3-0579-5660-a6f5-89767bdedcae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c822bdd5-b239-5109-a43e-980b6e32d798" :question/math-academy-id "q-278288" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/ba82c6b68026b0f4339c1e4fa01c4b1aa1b7aee74b74176f81bba1c0e1de259f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s$ and $V$ divides $\\overset{―}{RS}$ in the ratio $RV:VS=6:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OV}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{RV}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ORS,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =\\overset{\\to }{RO}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-r+s \\\\  & =s-r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $RV:VS=6:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RV}=\\frac{6}{11}\\overset{\\to }{RS}=\\frac{6}{11}(s-r).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OV} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV} \\\\  & =r+\\frac{6}{11}(s-r) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{11}r+\\frac{6}{11}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88425eae-da1e-5874-8f7f-5814599109b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88425eae-da1e-5874-8f7f-5814599109b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359326} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb4368da-49aa-53a7-bdff-de6cab55dfa3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93b212bf-e43c-5f8b-986d-a351f1f28af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b212bf-e43c-5f8b-986d-a351f1f28af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359328} :answer-field/id #uuid "2658f1c6-32fa-5c81-bd3e-c4ca5b4b9f96" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31c5dcf7-a95c-5a0d-988e-4a401492f1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-322577" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/de33644bcad663e67dc19c687dc0ef959310a50ddb83203031e66b951700510b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v,$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=9:8.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OW}$ via the vectors $u$ and $v.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OW}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$u+$\n{{field-2}}\n$v$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=9:8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{9}{17}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{9}{17}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{9}{17}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}u+\\frac{9}{17}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6d36014-95d6-5ae9-8983-abbc423330f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359331} {:answer/id #uuid "c39eb2d6-8ee0-530a-80cf-a9c7ba8188f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d36014-95d6-5ae9-8983-abbc423330f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359331} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c25b3e-a234-5557-9a6b-4262608f0e13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f696e90-a20c-59e9-9177-7a17222ae933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f696e90-a20c-59e9-9177-7a17222ae933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359334} :answer-field/id #uuid "c43c23df-dac3-5284-9d66-a524ed990aaa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ead9698e-ea1a-5ec5-bd9f-d140024169d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-327078" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m∦n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1973594-5791-5183-ab7b-102383c8610b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1973594-5791-5183-ab7b-102383c8610b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359337} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e374bd-cbd6-509c-8ab7-9a77c0fd8b4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a0d4929-ab26-52b9-982b-7dd07fcfd164" :question/math-academy-id "q-367062" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/5a5fcb431f6019d26cd6a92d703bfd2f9393bd494b2a3834dfb7e0f2668df393.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/43fdebc1ce180542e3cfdaf3f19e27e7ac869bbc5e37da7380886bd3d817bfb7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$"}]}
